include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose, and the point is WHERE.
--
-- A port's requirement is not declared -- it is whatever the body does with it.
-- `inner` calls a method on `thing`, so `thing` needs an instance; `outer` only
-- passes its own `thing` along, so it inherits the requirement. That inheritance
-- is why the derivation runs to a fixpoint, which terminates because recursion
-- is refused and the call graph is finite.
--
-- Without deriving it, the mistake surfaced inside the splice:
--
--   line 2: unknown instance or definition 'n'
--
-- Line 2 is the INNERMOST template's header, two levels from the call, naming a
-- scalar that is perfectly good -- its being unusable as an instance is the
-- point, and the message did not say so. That is the error blooming inside
-- instantiation, which is what a C++ `concept` exists to stop; mereo needs no
-- new syntax for it because the body already says what it requires.
--
-- Ports stay structurally typed: `tests/progs/sibling_call` and the library's
-- own templates splice over any instance carrying the method they call, with no
-- shared base. This refuses only what could never work.
--
--   refused:  'outer' calls a method on 'thing', so it needs an INSTANCE

inner (thing, n) goes
  thing.read (buffer is 0, capacity is 0, count is n)
end

outer (thing, n) goes
  inner (thing is thing, n is n)
end

program goes
  n is 0

  outer (thing is n, n is n)

end
